The C code implements the AMP (Abstract Message Protocol) — a simple binary protocol for encoding/decoding a list of string arguments into a single buffer. The wire format is:[0m[0m
- 1 header byte: high nibble = version, low nibble = argc[0m[0m
- For each argument: 4-byte big-endian length prefix + raw data[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The key challenge was that the Rust interface uses [38;5;10mString[0m/[38;5;10m&str[0m for what is fundamentally binary data. Two helper functions bridge this:[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mbytes_to_string[0m — maps each byte to a Unicode code point (Latin-1 style), ensuring all byte values are representable[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstring_to_bytes[0m — reverses the mapping[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The trickiest part was [38;5;10mdecode_arg(&mut self) -> &str[0m — it must both mutate [38;5;10mself.buf[0m (to advance the cursor) and return a [38;5;10m&str[0m borrowing from it. The solution stores [38;5;10mremaining + arg[0m in [38;5;10mself.buf[0m and returns a slice to the trailing arg portion. This way the next call to [38;5;10mdecode_arg[0m reads from the remaining data at the front.[0m[0m
